          {
            "label": "Privileged Service Account",
            "description": "Build a stronger credential for a privileged service whose recovered hash could face fast offline guessing.",
            "dictionaryAttack": true,
            "hybridAttack": true,
            "bruteForceAttack": true,
            "guessesPerSecond": 10000000,
            "requiredCrackSeconds": 31536000,
            "maxTokens": 6,
            "memorabilityBudget": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      "persona": "Blackhat Hacker",
      "taskMessage": "<b>Felix Rane:</b> A throttled login and a stolen hash are not the same game, and people who treat them the same lose one of them. On the portal, a patient guesser gets a slow trickle of tries and still has to be lucky. With the hash, patience is irrelevant, because the machine simply grinds.\n\nBuild each replacement for the specific thing coming at it. I have sat on the other side of that grind, and I can tell you which mistakes make it short work.",
      "taskInstructions": "A round clears only when every attack meter for that account survives the required time, inside its token slot and memorability limits. Rare, unusual words buy far more resistance per memorability point than leet spellings, digit suffixes, or breach-dump favorites.",
      "taskQuestion": "Forge the replacement credentials.",
      "taskCompleteText": "You hardened both credentials against their relevant attack models. Generic complexity theater has been replaced with measurable resistance."
    },
    {
